## Part C — Agentic & autonomous development techniques

These make autonomous work **safe and self-correcting**. The principle: give the agent fast, objective feedback loops so it can verify itself instead of needing a human at every step. Several map to installed skills — use them.

### C1. Process discipline (workflow skills)
- **Brainstorm → Plan → TDD → Verify → Review** for any non-trivial feature.
  - `brainstorming` skill before designing a feature.
  - `writing-plans` / `executing-plans` for multi-step work; keep the plan in-repo.
  - `test-driven-development` for domain logic.
  - `verification-before-completion` before claiming done.
  - `requesting-code-review` / `receiving-code-review` before merge.
- Small, vertical slices over big-bang changes — each slice independently testable.

### C2. Guardrails that make autonomy safe
Fast automated feedback = the agent catches its own mistakes:
- **TypeScript strict** — the compiler is the cheapest reviewer.
- **Tests as executable spec** — especially state machines, proposal/budget logic, orchestration handoffs.
- **Explicit state machines** — behavior is checkable, illegal paths rejected, not "vibes".
- **Lint + typecheck pre-commit + CI** — block bad commits before they spread.
- **Schema validation at every boundary** — DTOs in, structured outputs out.

### C3. Agent-ready task design (also how this system itself works)
Every task — whether Claude executes it or the Local Runner does — must be **self-contained**:
- Clear title + description.
- **Acceptance criteria** (objective, testable).
- Input context (relevant files/docs) + expected output.
- Definition of Done.
This is *recursive*: the backend must store tasks in exactly this shape (per source docs), because its own Engineer Agent consumes them. Designing build tasks this way is practice for the product.

### C4. Reliable AI output (treat AI as an unreliable function, validate it)
- **Structured outputs** — require JSON conforming to a schema; **validate** (zod) before persisting. Reject/retry on malformed output.
- **Golden/shape tests** — assert output *structure* and required fields, not exact prose.
- **Versioned prompt templates** — prompts live in-repo, versioned; outputs reference the prompt version (traceability).
- **Idempotency** — generation jobs keyed so re-runs don't duplicate; store + reuse outputs (cost awareness).
- **Cost-aware model routing** — frontier models for high-value tasks, cheaper models for summaries.

### C5. Verification & evidence (never claim done without proof)
- Run the actual command and read the output before reporting success.
- `/health` + smoke tests + a runnable seeded flow.
- E2E test covering the core loop slice being worked on.
- Evidence before assertions — paste the passing command output.

### C6. Traceability for self-debugging
- **Audit events** + **structured logs** (JSON, correlation/request id) so failures are reconstructable.
- **`agent_runs`** capture input summary, output summary, status, logs ref — the agent's own black-box recorder.
- `systematic-debugging` skill for any failure: reproduce → isolate → fix → verify. No guess-patching.

### C7. Version control discipline
- Branch per feature; small commits; **Conventional Commits**.
- Use **git worktrees** (`using-git-worktrees`) to isolate parallel work.
- PRs small enough to review; CI green before merge.
- Commit/push only when the human asks (per harness rules).

### C8. Recovery & resumability
- Long jobs resumable; distinguish **failed / incomplete / blocked / cancelled / human-paused** (per source docs).
- Retries with backoff for transient failures (AI/network); dead-letter for poison jobs.
- Idempotency keys so a re-run is safe.

### C9. Human-in-the-loop is non-negotiable
- High-risk actions (external comms, accept proposal, confirm payment, delete code, push to prod, scope change, final delivery) **always** route through an approval gate — even when the agent is "autonomous". Autonomy applies to *preparation*, not *irreversible commercial/destructive acts*.

